{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/262065-geist-chronicles-krewes","id":262065,"title":"Geist Chronicles: Krewes","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Geist: The Sin-Eaters"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-09-21T05:42:58-07:00","id":262065,"title":"Geist Chronicles: Krewes","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Trebuchet MS\"><span style=\"font-size:21px\">Krewes</span></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">There is a common belief among the Bound: Everyone dies, what matters is what you leave behind. Iron rusts, marble erodes, a true memorial is in people. The lives that you have changed will in turn change others. Your touch will echo down history. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">It doesn't matter if this belief is right, wrong or a transparent attempt to sound impressively wise. Sin-Eaters tell this koan, and others, to explain a simple fact. Their powers work best when they bind each other together. (The Bound also say it doesn't matter if this explanation is right or wrong, it has more heart than a chalkboard full of mystical mumbo jumbo equations)</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Most Krewes begin simply. A group of Sin-Eaters are drawn together by a common factor. It could be a goal, usually a simple one like getting rich, or quite often by being the only Sin-Eaters in town. Most begin with a constitution of some sort, it\u2019s practically a necessity: If one prospective member is a Reaper or lets his Geist run around tormenting people and another is a Gatekeeper sworn to defend mortals from the dead. Well it\u2019s important to have that discussion early on. Living with Geists tends to push the Bound to unhinged personalities, you can\u2019t just throw several in a krewe without a plan. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">The Bound don\u2019t have to take it beyond a Constitution but most do. Both more experienced Sin-Eaters and Geists encourage a newly formed Krewe to \u201cmake it official\u201d with a Ceremony for the long term benefits. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Trebuchet MS\"><b><span style=\"font-size:17px\">The Krewe Binding Ceremony </span></b></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">To form a Krewe requires an Avernum Gate, knowing participants (coercion works, mind control or trickery will not), and mind altering substances. Products of the underworld are considered good luck; you can find psychedelic mushrooms growing in the Upper Reaches or take a draught from Urdabrunnr, The River of Fate. Alternatively LSD, magic mushrooms, peyote or even copious amounts of alcohol may be used. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">When the participants are well and truly under the influence each party manifests their Geists. Next they open the Gate. If all goes well no one will see the Upper Reaches, instead the Gate will open to a vast expanse that stretches into the distance. Fog banks, staircases and rivers are common. Some Sin-Eaters say they can see all the way to the bottom of the Underworld. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Looking at their Geist the Sin-Eaters will notice a cord that stretches through the Gate and into the distance, to complete the ceremony each takes their partner\u2019s cord and weave them together. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">That\u2019s all it takes, though Sin-Eaters have been known to jazz up the ceremony.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Joining an existing Krewe uses the same ceremony. One Founder or Status 4+ member is required to induct new members and the initiates weave their Geist\u2019s cord to their sponsor\u2019s. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Trebuchet MS\"><b><span style=\"font-size:17px\">The Evolution of a Krewe</span></b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Even a group of friends will over time develop rituals and traditions. In jokes, or an annual party. Krewes exaggerate this tendency. Firstly they do so because they have different, more extreme, pressures on them. Many groups of friends come from the same environment and so are shaped by similar forces, Krewes are part of two different worlds which cannot help but shape them differently to their mortal neighbours. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Secondly a Krewes little rituals and cultural quirks tend to have mystical weight to them. A Krewe might use a blood sacrifice to open the way to the underworld, one day after using a black chicken for no particular reason they have an unusually successful trip. The next time they go out of their way to get a black chicken and, it\u2019s not quite as good as the first time but it\u2019s still pretty good. There\u2019s high fives all round and the Krewe agree they\u2019ve discovered a mystical principle: Sacrificing black chickens to open the Underworld is good luck. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">They\u2019re right, too. What makes a Krewe so central to a Sin-Eater\u2019s culture is that it\u2019s traditions have power. Even if sacrificing black chicken\u2019s does not provide luck, when the Sin-Eaters believed it did they created a new tradition, and following that tradition will create luck. The cause is different, but the effect is the same.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Then sometime later the Krewe will come across another Krewe who believe sacrificing black chickens is offensive to El Pollo and brings a curse upon everyone nearby\u2026 it probably won\u2019t be any trouble. Sin-Eaters bump into contradictory mythologies all the time. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">On the whole the Bound are aware of the mystical effects behind their Krewe\u2019s traditions have. Few believe their rituals or mythology are the One True Way (and those guys are unpopular). Most Sin-Eaters intentionally create traditions based on what they want to be true, which is not to say they go around telling everyone that \u201cwearing this necklace, this one around my neck, every day makes you a god\u201d. A Krewe\u2019s mysticism is as much a brand identity as a source of power. The Bound don\u2019t respect anything without an air of mystery, morbidity and badassness. Also, you can\u2019t turn yourselves into Gods with granny\u2019s necklace, Krewes are powerful but not that powerful. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Instead most Krewes use a hodge podge mix of funerary rituals stolen from various mortal cultures, ideas from respected older Krewes that have been around long enough to be fair game, old superstitions, and things that worked once and seem worth trying again. Krewes don\u2019t need to be based on genuine occult principles, but they don\u2019t hurt. Funerary rituals or odd tricks that worked before are good places to find the intersection of coolness and nuggets of genuine occult power. A few Krewes are dedicated to uncovering genuine occult truth, the advantages of using real occult principles usually balances out the loss of flexibility, usually. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">As a Krewe grows in size and age these effects increase. More Sin-Eaters weave their partner\u2019s cord into the Krewe\u2019s communal channel, creating a larger passage for the power of death. Discoveries and accomplishments of past Sin-Eaters remain past their death, widening the channel. Krewes have three different levels of power, which defines which Krewe Advantages they can purchase. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Mojo 1 - Old wives tales: Even the smallest and youngest Krewe can offer some power to it\u2019s members. Not too much, but at this level it\u2019s easy to experiment, to replace a bad tradition with something new. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Mojo 2 - Local traditions: As a Krewe grows it\u2019s power increases, but it becomes more rigid. Replacing an established tradition becomes serious work and those traditions can become restraining: Not only is sacrificing the black chicken good luck, not sacrificing it becomes bad luck. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Mojo 3 - Religious Dogma: At the apex of it\u2019s power a Krewe\u2019s rules become restrictive and cannot change without great effort and internal strife, but the power they offer is more than adequate compensation. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#666666\"><span style=\"font-family:Trebuchet MS\"><b><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Sidebar - Mojo and Tier</span></b></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">A Krewe\u2019s Tier, representing its general size, power and influence does not always match up with it\u2019s Mojo. A small dedicated Krewe of who constantly study, refine and add new elements to their channel could eventually reach Religious Dogma without ever growing beyond ten members. Though it must be said such small Krewes often take generations to accomplish this feat. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">You can usually assume that Mojo will be equal or higher to a Krewe\u2019s Tier, but a large sprawling Krewe with little interest in occult power may break this rule. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">For the record the population figures of the three Tiers look like this: </span></span></span><ul><li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Tier 1, At most 10 Sin-Eaters and up to thirty mundane members. </span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Tier 2, Up to 100 Sin-Eaters maybe as much as 500 mundane members. Most Krewes this size consist of autonomous tier one sized groups but this is not compulsory. </span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Tier 3, Over 100 Sin-Eaters and thousands of mundane members spread across two or more continents. </span></span></span></li> </ul><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#666666\"><span style=\"font-family:Trebuchet MS\"><b><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Krewe Benefits</span></b></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Krewes aren\u2019t just the people who have your back in the underworld. They provide powerful benefits to their members. There are three categories of benefits. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Founder - Only the founder of a Krewe or their successors can acquire this benefit. Some Founder Benefits can be inherited at no cost, these are labeled explicitly in the description.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Supernatural - Any Sin-Eater who has been Ceremoniously initiated into the Krewe can acquire this benefit. However the Krewe itself must buy the Merit first to unlock the potential. Some Supernatural benefits can apply to other kinds of Supernatural members, such as Mediums or Stoners. These are clearly marked in the text. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Temporal - Anyone, even a mortal, who is officially part of the Krewe can benefit. Sometimes people with unofficial membership can acquire the benefit. It\u2019s not supernatural, merely the results of access to the metaphorical communal coffers. </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">In addition there are three ways a benefit can be priced: </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Automatic - The benefit must be purchased by the Krew\u2019s founders, once they do so all qualifying members get the rewards. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Merit - The benefit is priced as a merit and each qualifying member must buy the benefit individually using experience. Most of these benefits are Founder benefits or have a Founder benefit as a prerequisite which defines how the benefit manifests in this Krewe. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Opt-In - The benefit must be purchased by the Krew\u2019s founders, afterwards any qualifying member can choose to gain the benefits, and it\u2019s drawbacks, or choose to ignore it. Social pressure may \u201cencourage\u201d members to partake. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#666666\"><span style=\"font-family:Trebuchet MS\"><b><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Krewe Experience</span></b></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Sin-Eater take a Krewe Beat every time they do one of the following actions. These may be spent on the various Benefits that a Krewe provides: </span></span></span><ul><li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Ethos - The character performed an action considered justified by the Krewe\u2019s rules or avoided one forbidden by the collieries to the Krewe\u2019s rules when it caused a significant problem or required a significant amount of effort. </span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lore - The character explored the Krewe\u2019s lore and learned something significant. For example, a Sin-Eater in a Krewe that believes you must always sacrifice to Mictlantecuhtli before entering the Underworld may experimenting with different sacrifices and record the success or failure of each expedition before concluding that Mictlantecuhtli likes black goats the best. Whether this is a valid discovery or a coincidence, or whether scholars of Aztec religion know damn well that Mictlantecuhtli prefers someone impersonating him to be sacrificed with incense does not matter. You still get the Beat and add black goats to your channel. </span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Lore (again) - Discovering an genuine occult mystery about death or the Underworld and adding it to the Krewe Channel grants you one beat. Be it a local mystery or a wide-reaching aspect of metaphysics. If you explore your Krewe\u2019s Channel and learn something useful then you gain both beats for Lore.</span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Loyalty - A Sin-Eater who is loyal to her Krewe in the face of adversity takes a Beat. </span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Zeal - When a player fulfills an Aspiration, and that Aspiration is directly tied to the Krewe, and it doesn\u2019t fall into one of the previous categories, take a Krewe Beat in addition to the regular Beat. </span></span></span></li> </ul><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Sidebar: Skipping to the Good Stuff</span></b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">If everyone knows that the players are going to found a Krewe, it is recommended that storytellers award Krewe Experience before the players get around to performing the Krewe Binding Ceremony. This is to avoid giving mechanical incentive to rush the character's natural development from strangers to trusted allies. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">If you\u2019ve accumulated a significant amount of Krewe Experience before you form a Krewe. You can declare that your characters received a mystic vision when performing the Krewe Binding ceremony, or that they think they did - psychedelics are part of the ceremony, and spend the exp on what was revealed to them. Naturally you should only do this if a religious vision fits your characters. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#666666\"><span style=\"font-family:Trebuchet MS\"><b><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Mojo One</span></b></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Krewe Mythology (OOO)</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Temporal Benefit, Automatic</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Why doesn't everyone Pass On? Why is the Underworld such a pit? Why do Sin-Eaters even exist? A Krewe\u2019s mythology is a story that explains the mysteries common to the Bound, and maybe a few more esoteric mysteries relevant to the Krewe\u2019s interests. It is common for a Mythology to draw connections between life and death, for example a Krewe formed from the victims of a war might speak of a devil figure whose misdeeds are responsible for both the war and the state of the Underworld. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">A large part of any Krewe is it\u2019s brand image and mythologies are the more image focused than most other Krewe Benefits. The myth\u2019s symbols and icons serve the same role as a corporate logo or gang colours. A deity or hero figure can show the outside world how the Krewe want their members to be perceived. Lurid tales of the devil\u2019s punishment could serve as a warning that the Krewe\u2019s enemies should expect no mercy. The elements incorporated from mortal cultures can show the Krewe\u2019s background and their pride in their religion, race, or community. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Most Mythologies are designed from the ground up as branding; the elements are designed to present an image or just to be cool or badass and spread that coolness to the more symbolic elements. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Sin-Eaters often customise their Momentos, reworking them in the styles or adorning them in the images of their mythologies. If they do so treat the Momento as one dot higher when measuring it\u2019s effects as a social bonus. Even being a part of a Krewe shows that you\u2019ve got Sin-Eaters watching your back, that\u2019s worthy of respect. For particularly large Krewes (one where it\u2019s possible for two members to not know each other) treat Momentos as two dots higher when measuring the social bonuses against fellow members. </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">In addition, when it is applicable, each Momento decked out in the Krewe\u2019s image gives a +1 to recognise your Krewe affiliation, to a maximum of +3. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">It is of course quite easy to deck out Momentos in another Krewe\u2019s style. There\u2019s nothing supernatural about paint. Some Krewes consider imitation the sincerest form of flattery, others are violently protective of their image. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#666666\"><span style=\"font-family:Trebuchet MS\"><b><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Sidebar: Fitting them together</span></b></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">It should be obvious that there are links between Krewe Mythology, Krew Ethos, Geist Aspects and Krewe Metaphysics. A Mythology or Metaphysic can justify the Ethos. The Aspects probably have a starring role in the mythology. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">There are no mechanical rules for this. You do not have to buy Mythology before Aspects. There\u2019s not even any rule against inconsistencies; but take some time to think how they fit together, perhaps plan all four out in advance. Designing a Krewe is part of the fun. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Kewe Ethos (O - OOOOO)</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Temporal Benefit, Opt In</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">What does the Krewe believe is moral? What do they believe is wrong? Is a medium who calls up Ghosts for grieving family offering closure, or violating the sacred border between life and death? </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Sin-Eaters can\u2019t rely on what they heard in school, the ethics of destroying a dangerous Geist aren\u2019t part of any curriculum. Without guidance, and usually without accurate occult information, Krewes tend to make their own moral code. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">For each dot in Krewe Ethos choose one moral rule, ideally one with a clear corollary stating something is immoral. These should also be specific actions, an ethos shouldn\u2019t say anything goes if it makes you money or satisfies the Geist. Some examples: </span></span></span><ul><li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Violence is ok to stop mortals breaching the barrier between life and death. Corollary: it is wrong to help mortals breach the barrier. </span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">A clean death isn\u2019t worse than survivable harm, we know you can pick yourself up afterwards. Corollary: A messy death that prevents you from moving on is a heinous crime. </span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Drugs should be legal, how you live is more important than how long you lived. Corollary: Drugs should be fun, pushing a bad trip just isn\u2019t right. </span></span></span></li> </ul><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Members of the Krewe may Opt-In to the Ethos (it\u2019s all or nothing). Every time they hit an Integrity Breaking Point they gain +3 if the Ethos said it was ok, but -3 if the Ethos said this is immoral. Mortal members get +2 or -2 instead. As always, succeeding on a Breaking Point roll can toughen you up until you no longer need to roll for that action, though it may take a few times for some of the things Krewes tend to talk about. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Note that mortal members of a Krewe can become ok with killing, under the circumstances permitted by the Ethos. It takes a lot for a mortal to become inured to the horrors of killing, but buying into the dogma of a freaky death cult can do it. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Geist Aspect (OO)</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Founder Benefit, Merit </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">What does the Geist represent? If your Geist is the Archangel Zadkiel, the Archangel of Mercy. Why was Mercy important enough to birth a Geist? What is the role of Mercy in the cosmos? What fundamental principles of the universe revolve around Mercy? </span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Perhaps Mercy is The Way To Heaven: Ghosts can escape the purgatorius underworld by showing Mercy. Perhaps mercy is the original sin, in the beginning there was no death but there was pain. The first murder was committed out of mercy to free someone from unbearable pain and that mistake cursed the universe with death for all time. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">All Aspects consist of two parts: The truth and the expression. The truth is normally a simple thing: \u201cHades is the benevolent lord of the dead\u201d or \u201cMercy is the Original Sin\u201d. The expression shows how the Geist relates to it, here are some possible expressions for \u201cMercy is the Original Sin\u201d:</span></span></span><ul><li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">The Good Side of Mercy - Your Geist symbolises why mercy is not fundamentally wrong even when it caused such suffering. It is the other side of the same coin, so to speak. </span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">The Bad Side of Mercy - Pick something wrong with mercy, your Geist represents that. </span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Selfishness - In the story mercy helped one person at a terrible cost to the universe. Your Geist represents selfishness or unwitting selfishness. It is ok to dip into the Krewe Mythology behind the truth in this manner. </span></span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">The victim - Your Geist is symbolic of someone who really needs mercy, but giving it mercy will have a terrible price. </span></span></span></li> </ul><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Aspects can be a complex intersection of morality, myth and personality. There is no proof that a Geist\u2019s Apsect actually is cosmologically significant, many Sin-Eaters think of Aspects as culturally significant rather than universal, in this case the culture is the Krewe, or think the whole thing is hogwash and only play along to keep their Geist happy. Others spend lifetimes examining the philosophical or theological implications of their Aspects, delving deeper into their Krewe\u2019s collective channel. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Whatever Aspects really are, the mechanical effects are simple. When your gain an Aspect you have an additional way to regain support. Depending on what you feel is appropriate an Aspect can function like a Geist\u2019s Passion or Past, either way you can regain support by symbolically acting out it\u2019s expression of the Aspect. For example if your Geist expresses its aspect of Mercy is the Original Sin as The Victim you could gain a point of support when somebody (including yourself) in the Geist\u2019s presence refuses to show the Geist mercy because it would hurt somebody else.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">The Geist doesn't have to like living out it\u2019s Aspect, you can get Support anyway and can even risk Synergy by helping the Geist go against a hated Aspect. (Presumably this represents your partner becoming less stable, even though it is grateful to you) </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Gaining an Aspect</span></b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">What happens when a Geist gains an Aspect: Sometimes, not much. The Archangel Zadkiel gains the Aspect \u201cMercy is the Path to Heaven\u201d and expresses it as \u201cThe Guy Who Shows Mercy\u201d. He looks at his Sin-Eater and says \u201cwhat were you expecting?\u201d. Nothing changed except that the player now has an extra way to gain Support points, which are mostly an out of character game system anyway. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">However a Geist taking the form of a merciless demon who gains that exact same Aspect and expression might physically transform into Zadkiel and claim the Sin-Eater redeemed him, or just deny he was a demon five minutes ago. Geists lie all the time, one reason Sin-Eaters try not to get dogmatic about Aspects. It doesn't have to be Zadkiel, he might transform into a human judge and start talking about mercy. He might not transform say that God showed up and cursed him to start showing mercy. He might claim he was always Zadkiel and merciful while fondling his cat-o-nine-tails and keep a straight face through such a blatant lie. He might claim he is an aspect of Zadkiel in the form of a demon. In short the possibilities are endless. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">It is just as likely that the demon might demonstrate a different expression of that Aspect: He might be the guy who was damned to hell by not showing mercy, or he might express the Aspect by being the guy who needs to accept mercy most of all. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">In short, gaining an aspect is an extremely flexible system. It can change everything about the Geist or nothing at all, so whatever your character concept is an Aspect should reinforce rather than clash with it. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#666666\"><span style=\"font-family:Trebuchet MS\"><b><span style=\"font-size:16px\">Sidebar: Morbid Passions</span></b></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">So if you have a Geist with Morbid Passions, you can just get an Aspect and use that to keep your support up and never worry again. Right? </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">Well you can, if you want to, but it\u2019s not that easy. A Sin-Eater can\u2019t just say \u201cI\u2019m an Advocate, so I\u2019ll have my Geist express \u2018The Ferryman transports souls to the afterlife\u2019 by becoming the Ferryman and supporting me in helping Ghosts Pass On.\u201d On some level the Geist has to accept that the Aspect reflects its role in the cosmos. It doesn't have to like it, it just needs to accept it. If you\u2019re enjoying playing out your struggle with geist of pure evil, say it\u2019s too stubborn to accept any aspect that isn\u2019t as horrid as it is and keep the Morbid Passion rules in play. You can say it gets that Aspect if that makes your Krewe\u2019s mythology cooler but a Morbid Aspect has no mechanics or cost. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">If playing the fight with your Geist isn\u2019t as fun as you thought it would be, an Aspect makes a neat explanation for fixing it without contradicting the past. You can even say the Aspect changed your Geist\u2019s regular Passion too. Even if the fight was fun to roleplay, convincing the Geist to accept an Aspect can be a fitting conclusion to the story. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Trebuchet MS\"><b><span style=\"font-size:17px\">Inheriting Aspects</span></b></span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">When a founder passes on the mantle to a successor their Geist takes on the Aspect. This is non-negotiable, either the Geist takes the Aspect, the mantle goes to the next guy in the line of succession or the founder\u2019s mantle is lost forever. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">What this means is that the successor\u2019s Geist expresses the same truth, but it may express it differently. For example, if a Krewe has the aspect \u201cHades is the totally awesome lord of the dead\u201d the founder\u2019s Geist may express this as \u201cI\u2019m Hades, regal and awesome!\u201d The founder gains Support by being an awesome leader. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">His successor gains the same Aspect, but her Geist expresses it in the opposite way: \u201cI\u2019m Hector, the challenger to Hades\u2019 rule for all rulers are challenged\u201d. The successor gains support whenever she\u2019s in a situation where her Geist challenges Hades rule (whether she supports or opposes that challenge). By the way, as Hector is merely an expression of the underlying truth, that Hades wears the crown, he can\u2019t actually succeed in overthrowing the lord of the dead. Though the Krewe might not survive the conflict. </span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:15px\">The duality between changeable expressions of unchanging truths reflects the duality of the Bound. It\u2019s the contrast between the forward looking, changeable, living Sin-Eater and the Geist who is bound to the past and only changes with outside influence. A Krewe can change with the times as new leaders express their Aspects in contemporary ways, but it always retains a link to the past and the truths it\u2019s founders uncovered. This may be the only real truth enshrined by Aspects. </span></span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":134,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","user":"Th